  "Area": "Unclassified",
  "Number": 16,
  "Question": "The sum of the digits of a two-digit number is subtracted from the number. The units digit of the result is \\(6.\\) How many two-digit numbers have this property?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(5\\)",
    "B": "\\(7\\)",
    "C": "\\(9\\)",
    "D": "\\(10\\)",
    "E": "\\(19\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "Let \\(a\\) be the tens digit and \\(b\\) the units digit, so \\(1\\le a\\le9\\) and \\(0\\le b\\le9\\). The number is \\(10a+b\\), and subtracting its digit sum gives\n\\[\n(10a+b)-(a+b)=9a.\n\\]\nThe units digit \\(b\\) cancels, so only the tens digit matters. As \\(a\\) runs from \\(1\\) through \\(9\\), the possible results are\n\\[\n9,18,27,36,45,54,63,72,81.\n\\]\nOnly \\(36\\) ends in \\(6\\), so \\(9a=36\\) and \\(a=4\\). Every units digit from \\(0\\) through \\(9\\) is then allowed. Thus exactly the numbers \\(40,41,\\ldots,49\\) work, giving \\(\\boxed{10}\\) numbers, choice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
